WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:04.730
JEN BENNETT: Welcome
to Cloud OnAir,

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:06.830
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:09.140
We're hosting webinars
every Tuesday.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:11.120
My name is Jen Bennett,
technical director

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:12.320
and the office's CTO.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.530
And I'm thrilled to be joined
by Jon Sobel, the CEO of Sight

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:18.144
Machine, one of Google
Cloud's partners.

00:00:18.144 --> 00:00:20.060
Today, we're going to
be talking about getting

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:23.480
manufacturing companies started
on digital transformation.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.790
Just as a reminder, you
can ask questions any time

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:26.820
on the platform.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.370
And we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:32.229
So let's get started.

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:35.220
So manufacturing is going
through a revolution.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.310
We hear about industry
4.0, smart manufacturing,

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:41.170
whatever the name might be.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:44.670
But the reality is the same
kind of revolution that we saw,

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.160
the digital revolution that
we saw in the consumer space

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.222
when we connected billions
of people and devices,

00:00:50.222 --> 00:00:52.680
is the same sort of revolution
that we're going through now

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.900
within the manufacturing space.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.090
And at the core of
that revolution,

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.576
it's really about
personalization.

00:00:59.576 --> 00:01:01.200
And we're starting
to see that now come

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.040
to life in manufacturing
in mass customization

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.640
and being able to personalize
products and services.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.180
And so with that, we look
at spaces like consumer

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.130
where we've seen some
of the technology that

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:18.270
evolved to adapt and to be able
to make that mass customization

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:19.410
affordable.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:21.480
And so we're going
to talk a little bit

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.970
about the technology, but it's
not just about technology.

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:27.870
It's also about people,
the process, the culture.

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:29.910
And at the core
of this technology

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:33.420
and this transformation,
it's really about data.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:34.920
And part of the
cultural shift is

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.250
becoming a really
data-driven organization

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.580
and being able to leverage the
data to drive personalization

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:42.990
and customization.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:45.360
And so with that, I'm going
to hand it over to Jon

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:46.950
to give a talk to
us a little bit more

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.110
about what they're seeing
in this space and some

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:50.880
of the technology that
you guys have built

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.760
to enable manufacturers
to go on this journey

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.440
of digital transformation.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.190
JON SOBEL: Thank you, Jen.

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.760
I'm going to share a little
background about data

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:03.150
in the manufacturing industry
and then, as Jen suggests,

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:06.420
talk a little bit about how
companies who are effectively

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:09.000
using it are doing so.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.140
First off, I think many of our
guests will appreciate this.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.330
Those of you in manufacturing
know this to be true.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:18.250
Manufacturing generates more
data than any other industry.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:21.720
We think of digital
data as fuel,

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.580
as an opportunity for insight.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.830
There is twice as much
data in manufacturing

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:29.430
as in any other
category in business.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:33.210
And, as those of you who
work in factories know,

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:34.110
this is what we do.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:35.568
This is how we make
factories work.

00:02:35.568 --> 00:02:37.830
You see the picture
of the white board.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:41.040
It's incredibly frustrating to
manufacturers to have so much

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.200
digital data available--
the data that's generated

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.160
by automation, by all the
controls and software that you

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:48.960
find in a factory--

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.750
and the best we've been
able to do in most places

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:55.260
is the daily or weekly scrum
and the white board session.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:56.490
And we do a pretty good job.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:57.510
Factories run very well.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.030
They make complicated
stuff effectively.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.610
But there's so much opportunity
we can use the data that's

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:03.690
being generated.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.210
And that's really the focus of
the technology behind Google

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:09.450
Cloud and what companies
like us are doing.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:12.960
A little bit more
landscape here--

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:17.820
data is useful and going
to be useful in a way that

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.490
really goes to the heart of
the future of manufacturing.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:25.050
We think about the United States
as a manufacturing economy.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:28.830
We benefited after World War
II from a temporary aberration.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.890
The great industrial
economies of the world

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:33.690
were sidelined by World War II.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.000
Germany and Japan
both spent 30 years

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.230
rebuilding their economies.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:40.830
And in the years since, China
has become the world's largest

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.180
manufacturer.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:47.010
So there is a global contest
for manufacturing leadership.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:50.550
And companies and countries
are investing heavily

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:53.490
in the next wave of innovation,
which is about data.

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:56.190
And data is useful for
every level of the game.

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:59.010
At the most basic
and the daily level,

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:01.560
the things that manufacturers
think about every day--

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.300
getting visibility
into your operations

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:06.390
into your supply chain,
keeping your factories

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:08.760
running as planned,
getting efficiency,

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.850
and getting control of quality--
these are perennial problems

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:14.440
that data is incredibly
effective for using.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:16.440
And then if we think about
everything else going

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:21.060
on-- robotics, 3D printing,
sustainability-- data

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.330
informs all of this.

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:24.787
And at the highest
level, the transformation

00:04:24.787 --> 00:04:26.370
that companies are
going through-- you

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:27.744
see some of the
leaders out there

00:04:27.744 --> 00:04:31.560
talking about manufacturing
as a service, micro factories.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.810
All of this depends on
having control of data.

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:35.220
JEN BENNETT: You know,
Jon, you talked about data.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:36.967
And we talked about
the volume of data.

00:04:36.967 --> 00:04:38.550
But really, the
problem's only getting

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.890
worse because as
Gartner had measured,

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:45.690
you have seen about 8.4 billion
devices connected in 2017, up

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.110
31%.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:50.220
But we're seeing an even larger
growth coming in the future

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:51.529
by 2020--

00:04:51.529 --> 00:04:53.820
the 20 billion devices,
something on the order of that.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:56.820
So this data that you're talking
about is only getting bigger.

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:57.390
JON SOBEL: You're right, Jen.

00:04:57.390 --> 00:04:58.890
I'm looking in the
rear-view mirror,

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:00.940
and the problem's only
going to get worse.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.370
And so we need to use this data.

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:05.897
In contrast to other
categories, you

00:05:05.897 --> 00:05:07.980
see things like smart
cities everyone talks about.

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:09.420
We've got to sensor
up the cities.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:11.810
There's already plenty
of data in manufacturing.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.310
And there's a really interesting
dynamic going on right now.

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:17.490
A couple of years ago, the
real question on people's minds

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:19.180
was, should we do this?

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:20.580
Does it matter?

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:22.290
Companies have
decided that they're

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.270
going to invest in digital.

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:27.360
98% of enterprises
are investing.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.890
They're doing things.

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:30.600
They're not going as
fast as they want,

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.634
and the question is why.

00:05:32.634 --> 00:05:34.050
We work with some
great companies.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.591
We work with a lot of companies
who are just getting started.

00:05:36.591 --> 00:05:37.960
And we see three themes.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:39.920
The first is the technology.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:43.030
It's not as easy
as everybody says.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:46.350
The effective use
of data requires

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:47.440
some real technical chops.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:49.260
And so you need
partners like Google

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.210
and some of the great
emerging companies

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:52.650
out there to use the data.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:54.800
That's a solvable problem.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:56.820
The companies that
serve manufacturers

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:59.320
are themselves trying to figure
out how to get good at this.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:01.590
So you've got to figure
out the business landscape.

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:05.040
And then perhaps the
most important variable

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:08.900
is the clients themselves--
the organizations.

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:10.740
Working with data
involves change.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:14.510
And the most important
thing is operationalizing

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:18.260
this technology,
getting new ways

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:20.090
of working into your workflow.

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:23.410
And that's where companies are
either succeeding or failing.

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:27.670
It's all about the organization.

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:29.740
Here's some interesting
statistics on how long

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:32.050
companies are spending piloting.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:36.070
This is from the World
Economic Forum and McKinsey.

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:38.860
More than 80% of companies
that are doing pilots

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:41.064
are spending a year or more.

00:06:41.064 --> 00:06:43.480
And in a quarter of cases,
they're spending two years just

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:45.187
to pilot technologies.

00:06:45.187 --> 00:06:47.020
Meanwhile, the companies
that are going fast

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:50.500
are going really fast, and
the gap between the leaders

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:53.625
and laggards is getting wider.

00:06:53.625 --> 00:06:55.000
To give you a
sense of the scale,

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:57.980
we work with a lot of
enterprise manufacturers.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:01.720
They're targeting $100
million a year-plus impact.

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:03.460
This is something
that can really make

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:04.752
a difference if you do it well.

00:07:04.752 --> 00:07:06.335
And one of the things
we'll talk about

00:07:06.335 --> 00:07:08.350
is how to do that, how
to be effective at this.

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:09.130
JEN BENNETT: One
quick question--

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:11.375
are you suggesting that
pilots aren't important?

00:07:11.375 --> 00:07:12.250
JON SOBEL: No, sorry.

00:07:12.250 --> 00:07:13.280
Pilots are important.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.030
They're important as
a way to get started.

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:17.297
And you and I both know no
manufacturer is just going

00:07:17.297 --> 00:07:18.380
to go to scale right away.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:20.410
They got to try something.

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:22.840
But there is a difference
between just trying stuff

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:26.020
to try it and trying stuff
with a purpose and a plan.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:29.950
And what the really effective
partners are insisting on

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:32.960
is that when you sit down
and plan out a program,

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:35.080
you get everybody around
the table-- operations

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:40.060
leaders, IT leadership,
corporate leadership-- and talk

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:43.901
about when this succeeds
or when we learn something,

00:07:43.901 --> 00:07:44.900
what are we going to do?

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:46.390
How are we going to scale it?

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:48.390
JEN BENNETT: I think
that's a really good point.

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:50.680
What we've seen as well
is that it's not just

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:52.471
about technology for technology.

00:07:52.471 --> 00:07:54.970
It's about technology solving
a really key business problem.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:55.900
So good point.

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:56.650
JON SOBEL: That's one
of the great things

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:58.025
about the
manufacturing industry.

00:07:58.025 --> 00:07:59.740
Our company is a
bunch of web heads

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:00.940
who grew up in the Midwest.

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:03.310
And most of us worked in
manufacturing growing up.

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:05.470
So we appreciate both worlds.

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:08.560
The great thing about
manufacturing for a technology

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:12.260
company is it's a skeptical,
practical industry.

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:13.550
It's about results.

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:15.850
It's about measuring things
and getting things done.

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:17.350
So you got to start
with what are we

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:19.516
going to fix, what are we
going to help make better,

00:08:19.516 --> 00:08:20.915
and then work back to the tech.

00:08:20.915 --> 00:08:22.540
Here's the technology
problem, and I'll

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:24.400
set this up really quickly.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:27.430
Big data is often said
to mean the three Vs--

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:29.830
volume, velocity, and variety.

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:34.059
Your companies have limitless
amount of computing power.

00:08:34.059 --> 00:08:35.770
You can handle any
volume of data,

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:38.086
any velocity of calculation.

00:08:38.086 --> 00:08:39.669
But the variety is
the killer problem.

00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:43.480
And manufacturing may be the
most varied data environment

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:44.800
that we find anywhere.

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:47.080
This is an example of
the kinds of equipment

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:48.700
you find inside of a factory.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:51.100
And it's just a thin
slice of what's out there.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:54.130
There's literally thousands
of data sources emitting

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:57.340
different kinds of data multiple
times a second all the time.

00:08:57.340 --> 00:09:01.270
Companies have done the hard
work of getting it aggregated,

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:02.229
getting it into a lake.

00:09:02.229 --> 00:09:04.186
But now, we've got to
figure out how to use it.

00:09:04.186 --> 00:09:05.950
And that's really the challenge.

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:09.082
One of the paradigms
that we all go

00:09:09.082 --> 00:09:10.540
to when we think
about this problem

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:11.581
is the software paradigm.

00:09:11.581 --> 00:09:15.130
We all say, oh, I must need
some new software to do this.

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:18.220
That probably means I
gotta rewire my factory.

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:19.930
I've got to change everything.

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:22.870
That's the last thing a good
manufacturer wants to do.

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:24.672
They've spent decades.

00:09:24.672 --> 00:09:26.380
Think about how hard
it is to make a car.

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:29.896
You spend decades
getting things dialed in.

00:09:29.896 --> 00:09:31.270
And the last thing
you want to do

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:33.340
is go change the control
software or the software

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:35.680
that you've relied upon.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:38.800
Good news-- this is not
really a software problem.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:40.360
This is a data problem.

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:42.480
Data is ones and zeros.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:44.630
It doesn't care where
it was generated,

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:47.970
and it doesn't really matter
what created the data.

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:50.940
What matters is getting
the data out and using it.

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:54.260
And with the advent of
technologies like AI and cloud,

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:55.670
it's a data paradigm.

00:09:55.670 --> 00:09:57.730
So what we need to do
to help manufacturers

00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:00.010
is get the zeros and
ones out and then

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:02.770
put them to work
with great tools that

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:05.520
can help us make sense of them.

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:06.890
If you do that well--

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:08.270
and this is very different.

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:10.162
If we go back for a
minute, the left side

00:10:10.162 --> 00:10:11.120
is a software paradigm.

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:12.330
It's an application paradigm.

00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:13.996
So everybody goes, I
need an application

00:10:13.996 --> 00:10:15.650
that understands this problem.

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:17.874
But data, when
used correctly, is

00:10:17.874 --> 00:10:19.790
effective for all kinds
of problems, even ones

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:21.630
we haven't thought of so far.

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:25.010
So if you think of the modern
manufacturing enterprise,

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:27.645
data is useful for everybody,
from the machine operator

00:10:27.645 --> 00:10:30.020
who's trying to figure out
how to make things work better

00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:32.990
during their shift all the way
up to corporate leadership.

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:35.300
We see a lot of companies
bringing on data scientists.

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:37.280
They're working with
process engineers.

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:40.490
Something as simple as evening
out the flow on a line,

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:41.835
getting rid of bottlenecks--

00:10:41.835 --> 00:10:43.460
couple of percentage
point improvement.

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:45.191
It drops right to
the bottom line.

00:10:45.191 --> 00:10:47.690
For an enterprise manufacturer,
we're talking about hundreds

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:49.430
of millions of
dollars a year just

00:10:49.430 --> 00:10:50.556
from using our data better.

00:10:50.556 --> 00:10:52.055
JEN BENNETT: Before
you move on, can

00:10:52.055 --> 00:10:53.300
you talk a little bit about--

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:55.049
you've talked about
these different roles.

00:10:55.049 --> 00:10:57.797
And some are on the IT side,
and some are on the OT side.

00:10:57.797 --> 00:10:59.630
Maybe you can give our
audience a little bit

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:01.790
of an idea about what
you're seeing in this.

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:04.116
And we talk about the
convergence of IT and OT,

00:11:04.116 --> 00:11:05.990
particularly in
manufacturing and around data

00:11:05.990 --> 00:11:12.350
and making data ubiquitous and
giving access to data in places

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:14.240
maybe where they
didn't have it before.

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:16.448
Give me a little bit of a
sense of what you're seeing

00:11:16.448 --> 00:11:17.729
in this IT, OT convergence.

00:11:17.729 --> 00:11:19.520
JON SOBEL: There's a
lot to say about that.

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:21.960
I'll try to be brief.

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:26.140
IT, for many years,
has served the carpet--

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:29.580
the business processes, the
front part of the operation.

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:33.820
And then there's been this
kind of interesting gray area--

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:35.480
the concrete.

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:39.060
There's often connectivity
on the plant floor,

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:40.710
and there's a ton of data.

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:43.330
But hasn't really been thought
of as the domain of IT.

00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:44.760
And so it's a bit
of a jump ball.

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:49.110
Who really should govern
and figure out how

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:50.520
to use data on the plant floor?

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:54.630
And so IT and OT teams are
starting to work together.

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:57.240
And they come at it from
very different perspectives.

00:11:57.240 --> 00:12:01.860
IT leadership has often been
focused on security and control

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:04.770
and having a reliable network,
and operations leadership

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:07.890
is focused on getting more
product out the door faster.

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:10.920
And good companies
are figuring out

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:12.540
how to put these teams together.

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:14.490
A lot of IT leadership
is really starting

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:16.170
to focus on how to
drive the needle

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:18.810
and be helpful to
the business in ways

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:21.780
that IT has never really been
called upon to do before.

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:23.730
And operations
leadership is starting

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:25.570
to learn about technology.

00:12:25.570 --> 00:12:27.460
It's very much a
work in progress.

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:28.770
We see all flavors of this.

00:12:28.770 --> 00:12:32.340
We see some companies where
IT leadership is really

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:34.127
pushing hard for change.

00:12:34.127 --> 00:12:36.210
We see some companies where
operations leaders are

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:38.220
basically saying to
their IT colleagues,

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:39.480
hey, if you don't get
this going for me,

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:40.854
I'm going to get
it going myself.

00:12:40.854 --> 00:12:41.710
I need to do this.

00:12:41.710 --> 00:12:44.730
And so it's going to be figured
out over the next couple years.

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:45.720
It's interesting.

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:47.219
JEN BENNETT: We
talk a lot in Google

00:12:47.219 --> 00:12:49.910
around this idea of
data democratization--

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:53.012
and now, even in machine
learning democratization.

00:12:53.012 --> 00:12:55.470
But what we've seen with some
of the manufacturers who have

00:12:55.470 --> 00:13:00.030
really adopted this idea of
democratizing data is that

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:01.960
people who it was
very difficult--

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:04.560
engineers who it was difficult
to get data from the shop floor

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:05.370
before--

00:13:05.370 --> 00:13:07.320
are now starting to be
able to get that data

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:09.900
and make better
decisions in their role.

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:13.200
Because of bringing in
that variety of data

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:15.000
to solve problems,
customer service

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:18.449
has been able to get better
data from the service side of it

00:13:18.449 --> 00:13:19.740
or from the manufacturers side.

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:22.500
So I think some
of this technology

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:23.970
is evolving to a
place where now we

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:26.460
can make this data
available to all roles

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:28.620
and help them
improve what they do

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:30.560
with that availability of data.

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:34.830
JON SOBEL: Well, and this is
why the cloud is so important.

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:38.520
Let's just talk
about all the kind

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:40.770
of functions you just listed.

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:42.540
Everything in a
manufacturing enterprise,

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:46.350
from design,
engineering, production,

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:48.870
the performance of
products out in the field,

00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:52.920
customer service, finance, sales
and monitoring-- zeros and ones

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:53.910
with all of that.

00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:57.360
And companies want to
understand all that together.

00:13:57.360 --> 00:13:58.960
Zoom out even one step further.

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:00.690
Let's talk about
the supply chain.

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:02.460
Why should the
boundaries of the firm

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:04.260
be the boundaries of
the data we look at?

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:06.070
It's all zeros and ones.

00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:09.079
And if we do this well, we
can serve these stakeholders

00:14:09.079 --> 00:14:11.370
and then everybody else who
might want to use the data.

00:14:11.370 --> 00:14:13.960
And I agree with you.

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:15.450
This is a strategic asset.

00:14:15.450 --> 00:14:17.264
This is not a
traditional IT problem.

00:14:17.264 --> 00:14:18.555
This is a strategic capability.

00:14:18.555 --> 00:14:21.690
If you can use your data
well, you got a real edge.

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:24.450
I give an example of a company
we started working with three

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:27.460
or four years ago that's
thinking exactly this way.

00:14:27.460 --> 00:14:30.380
And it was a real opportunity
for us as a young company.

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:33.359
We had been working with some
great automotive manufacturers

00:14:33.359 --> 00:14:35.400
who were very focused on
what to do in the plant.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:37.520
And this company
came to us and said,

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:40.450
we've got five
contract manufacturer

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:41.790
scattered all across Asia.

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:44.860
Each one's got their
own IT environment.

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:48.540
There was a bunch of automation
in each of these facilities.

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:51.660
Can you go wire up
this automation,

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:54.240
slurp off the data
multiple times a second,

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:56.040
and give us visibility
into everything

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:58.800
happening in this supply chain?

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:00.660
And we've done that
for them and others.

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:02.561
And this is how
companies of the future

00:15:02.561 --> 00:15:03.810
are going to think about data.

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:06.950
This is a root system
into their production.

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:09.600
Their contract manufacturers
get a view of what they do.

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:11.654
The OEM gets a
view of everybody.

00:15:11.654 --> 00:15:13.320
And everybody can be
better because they

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:15.160
can see what's going on.

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:17.294
Some other examples-- we
get asked all the time,

00:15:17.294 --> 00:15:18.210
how is this practical?

00:15:18.210 --> 00:15:19.920
What does it do for me?

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:24.420
We recently finished working
in one of the world's largest

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:26.700
dairy companies.

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:28.410
They make cheese.

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:31.784
And there is an artisan aspect
to the production of cheese

00:15:31.784 --> 00:15:34.200
that requires that you know
when to turn things on and off

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:35.850
in a very complicated process.

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:38.640
It's the same as if you're
making chemicals or oil.

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:40.950
There's a lot of wisdom
and tribal knowledge

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:43.110
about how to do this and
thousands of parameters

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:44.700
that have to be studied.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:47.220
If you can know exactly
when to do something

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:49.140
because the data
tells you, that's

00:15:49.140 --> 00:15:50.910
immediate impact
on the bottom line.

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:54.780
And this company expects to
realize 10% or 15% improvement

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:57.210
in the profitability
of this production

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:59.280
just because it's
using its data.

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:03.630
We've done some work recently
with huge amounts of robots.

00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:05.610
One of the largest auto
plants in North America

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:08.700
has 300 robots assembling cars.

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:11.100
The robots generate
a ton of data.

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:13.650
And if they go down-- and they
go down more than you would

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:15.459
expect-- it costs real money.

00:16:15.459 --> 00:16:18.000
So being able to know when is
that going to happen, how do we

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:19.810
stop it, how do we fix it--

00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:20.940
the data is all there.

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:23.319
If we can use it, we can get
control of these problems.

00:16:23.319 --> 00:16:25.860
JEN BENNETT: And you've given
us some examples of some pretty

00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:29.790
large manufacturing
and can often

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:33.570
be very expensive to do
these types of projects.

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:36.720
I'm wondering-- and I firmly
believe that this is not just

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:39.000
for the large enterprises.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:42.210
The technology has evolved to
a space where it's accessible.

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:43.980
So not only do we
have democratization,

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:45.590
it's accessible to
small to medium.

00:16:45.590 --> 00:16:48.090
Maybe can give us a little bit
of insight into that as well.

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:49.006
JON SOBEL: Absolutely.

00:16:49.006 --> 00:16:51.300
So a couple of comments there--

00:16:51.300 --> 00:16:54.165
we have some terms we
use inside our company

00:16:54.165 --> 00:16:55.800
to describe the fastest movers.

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:57.524
And we talk about gazelles.

00:16:57.524 --> 00:16:59.190
They're often not the
largest companies.

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:02.041
They're nimble.

00:17:02.041 --> 00:17:04.290
They want to use their data,
and they want to go fast.

00:17:04.290 --> 00:17:05.456
And you're absolutely right.

00:17:05.456 --> 00:17:08.560
This isn't just a game
for the biggest companies.

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:10.770
The other thing is
that technology really

00:17:10.770 --> 00:17:13.230
has been democratized now.

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:17.670
We can spin up an app
and map data to it

00:17:17.670 --> 00:17:20.089
and show you visibility
into your machines

00:17:20.089 --> 00:17:22.829
in literally an hour or less.

00:17:22.829 --> 00:17:24.750
That technology
is proliferating.

00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:27.390
And just to throw
around a few stats,

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:29.640
there's over a million
factories in the United States,

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:32.250
more than a million in China,
more than a million in Europe.

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:34.200
A very healthy
percentage of those

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:36.900
are owned by small and
medium-sized enterprises.

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:40.410
And the technology to do this
is available for every company

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:41.130
on that spectrum.

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:47.380
We spend a lot of time studying
the organizations we work with.

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:49.220
And I just wanted
to call this out

00:17:49.220 --> 00:17:53.610
in case you're interested
in assessing your company.

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:55.450
There is a lot of
talk about readiness,

00:17:55.450 --> 00:17:56.616
and it's really interesting.

00:17:56.616 --> 00:17:57.440
We go to companies.

00:17:57.440 --> 00:17:59.140
And one of the
things we've learned

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:00.910
to do-- when you
bring new technology

00:18:00.910 --> 00:18:03.910
into an organization, it's
important to think about

00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:05.410
how are we going
to succeed at this.

00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:07.090
Where should we start?

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:10.270
And we learned early on-- this
was an unexpected insight--

00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:12.730
that even within
the same company,

00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:14.150
every plant is very different.

00:18:14.150 --> 00:18:16.840
And so you have widely
varying levels of readiness

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:19.120
within the same organization.

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:22.180
You'll see on this chart
there is a kink to the left.

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:24.820
On the y-axis is
organizational readiness.

00:18:24.820 --> 00:18:28.450
On the x-axis is
technical readiness.

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:30.970
To our surprise,
what we learned was

00:18:30.970 --> 00:18:35.710
the very best plants were
led with the right mindset.

00:18:35.710 --> 00:18:36.760
They had a focus on data.

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:38.680
They valued data.

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:41.170
It didn't really matter what
their technical readiness

00:18:41.170 --> 00:18:44.170
was as much as the mindset
of the organization

00:18:44.170 --> 00:18:45.470
and the leadership.

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:47.770
And one of the best
plants we ever went into

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:49.040
was running on Excel.

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:50.680
They were running
on Excel, but they

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:52.700
understood the value of data.

00:18:52.700 --> 00:18:55.840
And so we developed
over a period of years

00:18:55.840 --> 00:18:58.090
and based on going
into hundreds of plants

00:18:58.090 --> 00:19:02.590
an index that helps you
score where your plants are

00:19:02.590 --> 00:19:04.330
and where to start.

00:19:04.330 --> 00:19:08.770
And if there's one take-home
point, it's pick situations--

00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:11.350
we talked about pilots
and getting things going--

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:15.010
pick situations where there's
some committed leadership

00:19:15.010 --> 00:19:16.224
at the right levels.

00:19:16.224 --> 00:19:18.640
There's so many people and
companies that have great idea.

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:22.900
The tradition of quality
of walking the genba

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:25.930
is that people in be plant
know more than anybody else

00:19:25.930 --> 00:19:27.980
about what to do and
how to make it better.

00:19:27.980 --> 00:19:31.090
So tap into that
wisdom and knowledge.

00:19:31.090 --> 00:19:33.340
Make sure you have plant
leadership aligned.

00:19:33.340 --> 00:19:34.930
And then strong
corporate leadership

00:19:34.930 --> 00:19:38.530
is so important to knock down
roadblocks and support people.

00:19:38.530 --> 00:19:40.780
There's all kinds of other
interesting cultural things

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:41.650
we might talk about.

00:19:41.650 --> 00:19:44.935
Transparency can be wonderful.

00:19:44.935 --> 00:19:47.560
If an organization really wants
to understand what it does well

00:19:47.560 --> 00:19:49.360
and what it can be
better at, transparency

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:50.810
is incredibly powerful.

00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:52.420
There are some
organizations that

00:19:52.420 --> 00:19:54.007
struggle with transparency.

00:19:54.007 --> 00:19:56.590
And so you have to really think
about where is the right place

00:19:56.590 --> 00:19:57.140
to start.

00:19:57.140 --> 00:19:58.764
How do we support
people when they find

00:19:58.764 --> 00:20:00.160
problems we didn't know we had?

00:20:00.160 --> 00:20:02.210
And how do we fix them?

00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:05.549
And that leads to a
list of best practices.

00:20:05.549 --> 00:20:07.090
There's a lot of
words on this slide.

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:10.410
I'll try to distill
the key ones.

00:20:10.410 --> 00:20:12.730
We talked about
pilots with a focus

00:20:12.730 --> 00:20:14.400
on getting to operational.

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:17.730
Making operational
use the acid test

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:20.220
is something that good
companies do right away.

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:23.040
It's one thing to try
technology just to experiment,

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:24.660
but we got to get going here.

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:29.391
And so thinking
about when this works

00:20:29.391 --> 00:20:30.890
or when we learn
something, what are

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:33.770
we going to do to go to scale
separates companies that

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:36.700
are succeeding from others.

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:38.400
This is a change exercise.

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:40.340
We've seen a few companies--

00:20:40.340 --> 00:20:43.140
another bullet point here
is about embracing change

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:45.240
as a discipline in itself.

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:46.050
I was shocked.

00:20:46.050 --> 00:20:51.660
We went to a plant
recently in Australia.

00:20:51.660 --> 00:20:54.150
And somebody introduced
herself and said,

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:56.000
I'm the change leader here.

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:57.330
I asked her, what's your job?

00:20:57.330 --> 00:21:00.510
She said, I was hired to
help the technology people

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:02.051
talk to the operations people.

00:21:02.051 --> 00:21:04.050
And I've seen this now
in two or three companies

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:05.258
in the last couple of months.

00:21:05.258 --> 00:21:07.080
Companies are actually
thinking about how

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:09.021
do we bring this
into our operations,

00:21:09.021 --> 00:21:10.770
how do we get people
talking to each other

00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:12.720
and have designated
change leaders.

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:14.890
JEN BENNETT: I think
that's really interesting.

00:21:14.890 --> 00:21:16.620
If you think about
lean manufacturing,

00:21:16.620 --> 00:21:21.240
it really is the foundation
of agile software practices.

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:23.040
And yet, we've
kind of gotten away

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:24.630
from this idea of adaptability.

00:21:24.630 --> 00:21:27.550
I know you and I have talked
about this quite a bit.

00:21:27.550 --> 00:21:29.790
But the world is
changing faster than

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:32.400
even our manufacturing
clients can adapt.

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:35.160
And so this idea of
adaptability and agility

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:37.100
is going to be more
important now than ever.

00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:40.170
And really, if you take
your core foundation--

00:21:40.170 --> 00:21:43.470
the ability to
automate the processing

00:21:43.470 --> 00:21:46.330
of data and bringing together
all of that variety of data

00:21:46.330 --> 00:21:48.330
to help make better
decision process, to support

00:21:48.330 --> 00:21:50.270
the decision making processes--

00:21:50.270 --> 00:21:53.850
you do enable agility in
ways that really, truly

00:21:53.850 --> 00:21:55.140
allow growth.

00:21:55.140 --> 00:21:57.540
JON SOBEL: I'm so
glad you said that.

00:21:57.540 --> 00:22:00.250
The notion of
continuous improvement,

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:03.420
which is the soul of
great manufacturing,

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:06.840
is exactly the same idea as
using data to get better.

00:22:06.840 --> 00:22:09.300
And great manufacturers
think about getting

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:10.950
a little bit better every day.

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:15.480
And just like lean,
this is another tool

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:16.800
in the toolkit for doing it.

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:17.560
It's powerful.

00:22:17.560 --> 00:22:21.084
It's powerful because once
you get that foundation set,

00:22:21.084 --> 00:22:22.500
you can do so much
more beyond it.

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:25.537
But we don't have to change
the soul of manufacturing.

00:22:25.537 --> 00:22:27.120
This is what
manufacturers do already.

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:29.770
They think, how do I
get better every day?

00:22:29.770 --> 00:22:34.140
The other thing I would suggest
is have a big, bold vision.

00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:35.190
Go big and fast.

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:36.900
Don't do everything upfront.

00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:39.030
But be thinking about
more than just production.

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:41.550
Be thinking about the links
between production data

00:22:41.550 --> 00:22:43.710
and everything else
in your organization.

00:22:43.710 --> 00:22:46.410
And last-- and we call this out
as a kind of a special bullet

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:47.340
point--

00:22:47.340 --> 00:22:49.620
partnering.

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:51.060
This is a comment
that's directed

00:22:51.060 --> 00:22:52.230
at the really large companies.

00:22:52.230 --> 00:22:54.271
I think a lot of manufacturers
partner very well.

00:22:54.271 --> 00:22:56.520
If you think about how
good supply chains work,

00:22:56.520 --> 00:22:59.820
those are essentially
partnerships.

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:02.100
But some very
large manufacturers

00:23:02.100 --> 00:23:06.870
are accustomed to working
with kind of a monopoly

00:23:06.870 --> 00:23:08.640
position in their industry.

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:10.680
I was speaking with
a CIO last week.

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:12.711
And I think he paid us
the greatest compliment

00:23:12.711 --> 00:23:14.960
and in a way said the greatest
thing about his company

00:23:14.960 --> 00:23:16.350
he could have said.

00:23:16.350 --> 00:23:17.980
We're talking about
working together.

00:23:17.980 --> 00:23:19.620
And I said, hey,
can I come see you?

00:23:19.620 --> 00:23:21.180
When can I get on your calendar?

00:23:21.180 --> 00:23:22.900
And he said, listen.

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:24.450
You come down here
whenever you want.

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:25.720
You're not a vendor.

00:23:25.720 --> 00:23:26.550
You're a partner.

00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:28.560
I'm trying to
change our company,

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:31.230
and the way I'm going to
do that is with partners.

00:23:31.230 --> 00:23:33.220
That truly is a mindset--

00:23:33.220 --> 00:23:35.300
if we think about
how procurement works

00:23:35.300 --> 00:23:37.905
and all the stuff that
we do in large companies,

00:23:37.905 --> 00:23:39.780
they're not built with
a partnership mindset.

00:23:39.780 --> 00:23:41.738
They're built with
efficiency and they're built

00:23:41.738 --> 00:23:42.840
to serve the organization.

00:23:42.840 --> 00:23:44.977
When we're talking about
change and using data

00:23:44.977 --> 00:23:46.560
and bringing in
technology, it's going

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:47.643
to be through partnership.

00:23:47.643 --> 00:23:50.370
And companies that are good at
partnering-- and one of things

00:23:50.370 --> 00:23:52.380
I admire so much about
Google is Google really

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:54.540
does partner with companies.

00:23:54.540 --> 00:23:58.650
You all have thought about
changing so many industries

00:23:58.650 --> 00:24:01.720
that you bring that
sensibility to the table.

00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:03.870
And those manufacturers
that are doing this well

00:24:03.870 --> 00:24:05.713
are definitely thinking
that way as well.

00:24:05.713 --> 00:24:09.540
So those are some learnings.

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:10.575
We ought to just--

00:24:10.575 --> 00:24:11.901
I'll take it from there.

00:24:11.901 --> 00:24:14.400
JEN BENNETT: One quick question
or thought that I had, too--

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:15.480
we talked about data.

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:17.670
And you talked about
the fact that people can

00:24:17.670 --> 00:24:19.180
handle large volumes of data.

00:24:19.180 --> 00:24:22.300
But what we find in practice is
that people are handling them

00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:26.640
through spreadsheets and through
notebooks and through word

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:27.270
of mouth.

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:29.610
And that's not really
how you handle big data.

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:31.710
Tell us a little bit
about some of the--

00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:34.610
I'm super excited about the
technology that we offer.

00:24:34.610 --> 00:24:36.875
But I'm even more
excited in some ways

00:24:36.875 --> 00:24:38.750
about some of the secret
sauce that you have.

00:24:38.750 --> 00:24:40.375
Can you give us a
little bit of insight

00:24:40.375 --> 00:24:42.104
into some of the secret sauce?

00:24:42.104 --> 00:24:43.020
JON SOBEL: Absolutely.

00:24:43.020 --> 00:24:44.700
I appreciate the question.

00:24:44.700 --> 00:24:46.950
I didn't want to dive
too deep into what we do

00:24:46.950 --> 00:24:50.289
or into the tech, but
here's what's been missing.

00:24:50.289 --> 00:24:52.830
If it were easy to do this, this
would have been done already

00:24:52.830 --> 00:24:54.371
because companies
have been wrestling

00:24:54.371 --> 00:24:57.420
with spreadsheets and data
lakes and data for years.

00:24:57.420 --> 00:25:02.310
What's been missing is what we
call a canonical data model--

00:25:02.310 --> 00:25:07.930
a systematic way to drop
into any manufacturing

00:25:07.930 --> 00:25:10.830
and environment and
make sense of that data

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:12.930
through an automated data model.

00:25:12.930 --> 00:25:16.227
And you need a model that can
handle different environments.

00:25:16.227 --> 00:25:18.060
You can't just build a
model for a car plan.

00:25:18.060 --> 00:25:19.710
Because then you could
understand part of a car plan,

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:21.418
but you can't go to
an electronics plant.

00:25:21.418 --> 00:25:24.770
So you actually need a model
manufacturing as a discipline.

00:25:24.770 --> 00:25:27.940
Bake that smarts
into your data model.

00:25:27.940 --> 00:25:28.980
And then what you find--

00:25:28.980 --> 00:25:30.115
so this was our journey.

00:25:30.115 --> 00:25:32.490
We started working in a bunch
of plants around the world,

00:25:32.490 --> 00:25:34.409
and they all made
different things.

00:25:34.409 --> 00:25:35.950
And we needed a way
to understand it.

00:25:35.950 --> 00:25:37.770
So we built
abstracted data models

00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:40.679
and kept refining and improving
them through experience.

00:25:40.679 --> 00:25:42.720
And then we had to pipe
in a huge amount of data.

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:45.720
Our company takes in about
15 billion data points a week

00:25:45.720 --> 00:25:46.840
right now for our clients.

00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:49.340
And that's a fraction of what
we'll do in a couple of years.

00:25:49.340 --> 00:25:51.360
And you need software
automation to put

00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:53.640
all that data in the
right place and to use

00:25:53.640 --> 00:25:55.380
machine learning
and classification

00:25:55.380 --> 00:25:57.420
techniques to understand it--

00:25:57.420 --> 00:25:59.970
so basically, having
a representation

00:25:59.970 --> 00:26:02.790
of what happens in a factory
built into math and software

00:26:02.790 --> 00:26:04.230
so you can get empirical.

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:06.090
And we couldn't have
done this 10 years ago.

00:26:06.090 --> 00:26:07.965
If you think of all the
different data types,

00:26:07.965 --> 00:26:11.090
there's time series
data, there's alarms,

00:26:11.090 --> 00:26:14.190
there's stuff in Excel,
there's text, there's pictures,

00:26:14.190 --> 00:26:15.920
we now have the
technologies that

00:26:15.920 --> 00:26:18.060
allow us to bundle and
attach all those data

00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:19.590
points to these models.

00:26:19.590 --> 00:26:21.499
And so that's
what's been needed.

00:26:21.499 --> 00:26:23.790
And if you put that in kind
of the middle of everything

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:27.240
and then you extract the data,
put it through that engine,

00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:29.640
and then, most important,
find relationships that

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:30.857
matter to a business person--

00:26:30.857 --> 00:26:32.940
we don't just want to give
people a bunch of tools

00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:34.106
and say, play with the data.

00:26:34.106 --> 00:26:36.670
We want to take it to,
hey, the machines down.

00:26:36.670 --> 00:26:37.380
Why?

00:26:37.380 --> 00:26:40.800
Or something bad happened
and caused bad quality.

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:43.590
What's different in the system
for bad parts and good parts?

00:26:43.590 --> 00:26:45.154
Or I got a recall.

00:26:45.154 --> 00:26:47.070
Do I need to recall
everything, or just things

00:26:47.070 --> 00:26:48.780
that were made last Thursday?

00:26:48.780 --> 00:26:51.480
The answers are there in
the data if we can use it.

00:26:51.480 --> 00:26:53.646
JEN BENNETT: You talked a
little bit throughout this

00:26:53.646 --> 00:26:55.710
about the cultural
change and the leadership

00:26:55.710 --> 00:26:57.420
and how that can really
help enable this.

00:26:57.420 --> 00:26:58.980
But I'm guessing a
lot of our viewers

00:26:58.980 --> 00:27:01.909
may not be the CEO
of their companies.

00:27:01.909 --> 00:27:03.450
Maybe give us a
little bit of insight

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:08.247
of how everybody in their
role can really take advantage

00:27:08.247 --> 00:27:09.330
of this and leverage this.

00:27:09.330 --> 00:27:12.120
How is this applicable
to our viewers?

00:27:12.120 --> 00:27:12.840
JON SOBEL: Sure.

00:27:12.840 --> 00:27:14.760
So I think it's an
important question

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:16.740
because one of the
comments we had

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:19.650
earlier on one of our slides is
the importance of leadership.

00:27:19.650 --> 00:27:23.784
Company leadership that
embraces this is critical.

00:27:23.784 --> 00:27:25.200
I really appreciate
Jen's question

00:27:25.200 --> 00:27:28.137
because not every leadership
team has necessarily

00:27:28.137 --> 00:27:28.720
embraced this.

00:27:28.720 --> 00:27:30.930
So what do you do if
you're in an organization

00:27:30.930 --> 00:27:34.870
and you want to start making
progress with your data?

00:27:34.870 --> 00:27:35.740
How do you do it?

00:27:35.740 --> 00:27:38.560
And I think that's the majority
of organizations today.

00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:41.370
So a couple of comments--

00:27:41.370 --> 00:27:45.080
first, pick something that
you can get started with

00:27:45.080 --> 00:27:45.920
and go fast.

00:27:45.920 --> 00:27:48.530
And it's amazing how
once a screen pops up

00:27:48.530 --> 00:27:51.470
and there's some evidence
that you can see something

00:27:51.470 --> 00:27:53.770
that you couldn't see
before-- we used to joke about

00:27:53.770 --> 00:27:55.437
in our earliest days,
we started working

00:27:55.437 --> 00:27:56.978
at a big automotive
plant in Detroit.

00:27:56.978 --> 00:27:58.190
And we called it factory TV.

00:27:58.190 --> 00:27:59.330
As soon as there
was a picture up

00:27:59.330 --> 00:28:01.490
and people could see what was
going on, they got excited.

00:28:01.490 --> 00:28:02.530
So you got to
initiate the change.

00:28:02.530 --> 00:28:03.390
JEN BENNETT: They got empowered.

00:28:03.390 --> 00:28:04.056
JON SOBEL: Yeah.

00:28:04.056 --> 00:28:04.940
Yeah, exactly.

00:28:04.940 --> 00:28:07.110
And they could
make a difference.

00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:09.670
So you got to
initiate the change.

00:28:09.670 --> 00:28:12.430
I think that,
speaking very bluntly,

00:28:12.430 --> 00:28:13.960
fear works really well--

00:28:13.960 --> 00:28:16.969
letting your leadership team
know you're falling behind.

00:28:16.969 --> 00:28:18.760
You're falling behind
as a management team.

00:28:18.760 --> 00:28:20.290
Here's what other
companies in our--

00:28:20.290 --> 00:28:21.520
you don't say it that way.

00:28:21.520 --> 00:28:24.310
But here's what other companies
in our industry are doing.

00:28:24.310 --> 00:28:27.310
And here's where we're going
to be if we don't keep up

00:28:27.310 --> 00:28:29.330
is effective.

00:28:29.330 --> 00:28:32.980
And then I think so much of
this is cross-functional.

00:28:32.980 --> 00:28:36.790
So finding champions-- you and I
had an interesting conversation

00:28:36.790 --> 00:28:41.530
before we started today about
command and control and how

00:28:41.530 --> 00:28:43.900
increasingly, organizations--

00:28:43.900 --> 00:28:47.620
Jamie Dimon just wrote a
great shareholder letter.

00:28:47.620 --> 00:28:50.650
JP Morgan CEO said,
bureaucracy is a disease.

00:28:50.650 --> 00:28:52.300
They need to get rid of it.

00:28:52.300 --> 00:28:52.990
It's amazing.

00:28:52.990 --> 00:28:56.240
Large companies are
realizing that to innovate,

00:28:56.240 --> 00:28:58.090
we've got to open
up the channels.

00:28:58.090 --> 00:29:01.030
And if you're somebody
who wants to make change

00:29:01.030 --> 00:29:03.070
happen, find allies.

00:29:03.070 --> 00:29:05.110
Find people who are influential.

00:29:05.110 --> 00:29:07.900
Pick a project that
is within your budget.

00:29:07.900 --> 00:29:11.545
Get it, light a spark, and
hopefully, people will pile on.

00:29:11.545 --> 00:29:13.420
JEN BENNETT: And I also
think this whole idea

00:29:13.420 --> 00:29:16.300
of democratization of data,
making data available--

00:29:16.300 --> 00:29:19.720
as a leader, that's a choice
you can make pretty easily--

00:29:19.720 --> 00:29:21.200
making data available.

00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:24.430
And then what we've seen, even
within our own operation--

00:29:24.430 --> 00:29:27.460
so we run data centers.

00:29:27.460 --> 00:29:29.560
And we're a data-driven
organization anyways.

00:29:29.560 --> 00:29:31.540
But we've had data availability.

00:29:31.540 --> 00:29:33.094
We had engineers
who took the data

00:29:33.094 --> 00:29:34.510
and built machine
learning models.

00:29:34.510 --> 00:29:37.690
And now, our data
centers in the summer are

00:29:37.690 --> 00:29:39.750
running 40% more efficiently--

00:29:39.750 --> 00:29:40.900
40%.

00:29:40.900 --> 00:29:44.020
I'm talking to manufacturers now
where every single percentage

00:29:44.020 --> 00:29:46.960
point is large
amounts of money--

00:29:46.960 --> 00:29:50.470
so 40% when you
make data available

00:29:50.470 --> 00:29:53.230
and empower the individuals
within the organization.

00:29:53.230 --> 00:29:57.150
So I think we have to
continuously champion

00:29:57.150 --> 00:29:59.980
the return on investment
that we see just by making

00:29:59.980 --> 00:30:01.240
data readily available.

00:30:01.240 --> 00:30:02.620
And as you said,
the secret sauce

00:30:02.620 --> 00:30:04.940
is making it usable as well.

00:30:04.940 --> 00:30:07.040
So I think that's
really important.

00:30:07.040 --> 00:30:09.460
I think we have just a couple
more minutes before we're

00:30:09.460 --> 00:30:12.010
going to start the live Q&amp;A.
So maybe one of the things

00:30:12.010 --> 00:30:13.300
I wanted to talk about--

00:30:13.300 --> 00:30:16.810
we talk a lot about analytics.

00:30:16.810 --> 00:30:18.700
And there's many, many
companies out there

00:30:18.700 --> 00:30:21.860
who are doing analytics.

00:30:21.860 --> 00:30:24.400
Why do we still lack
this basic visibility

00:30:24.400 --> 00:30:26.544
when these tools are
available and analytics

00:30:26.544 --> 00:30:27.460
are readily available?

00:30:27.460 --> 00:30:30.044
What is it that is
causing this problem?

00:30:30.044 --> 00:30:31.960
JON SOBEL: So analytics
is one of those words.

00:30:31.960 --> 00:30:33.237
It's a tough word.

00:30:33.237 --> 00:30:34.570
I think you're absolutely right.

00:30:34.570 --> 00:30:37.750
You asked me a few
minutes ago what's

00:30:37.750 --> 00:30:41.260
the secret sauce to
making data useful.

00:30:41.260 --> 00:30:44.830
If we can't put data in
the right building blocks,

00:30:44.830 --> 00:30:47.140
we can't do the math we
need to do on top of things.

00:30:47.140 --> 00:30:49.690
If I try to understand a bunch
of time series data points

00:30:49.690 --> 00:30:51.781
and then relate that to
thousands of other things,

00:30:51.781 --> 00:30:53.030
I'm not going to get anywhere.

00:30:53.030 --> 00:30:56.680
And so let's think
of analytics as math.

00:30:56.680 --> 00:30:58.840
There's no secret math
to understand a factory.

00:30:58.840 --> 00:31:01.810
We can use a lot of good
statistical techniques

00:31:01.810 --> 00:31:05.360
and machine learning
techniques on top of data.

00:31:05.360 --> 00:31:07.210
There's probably
hundreds of companies

00:31:07.210 --> 00:31:09.910
out there right now hand
waving in some form or another

00:31:09.910 --> 00:31:10.870
about analytics.

00:31:10.870 --> 00:31:12.869
But I'll tell you what's
been really interesting

00:31:12.869 --> 00:31:14.360
in our journey.

00:31:14.360 --> 00:31:15.820
The customers are smart.

00:31:15.820 --> 00:31:19.420
Manufacturers have been working
with statistics and with data

00:31:19.420 --> 00:31:22.420
for many years, and
they know whether or not

00:31:22.420 --> 00:31:24.860
something can
analyze their data.

00:31:24.860 --> 00:31:28.270
And so we try to stay away
from the software industry

00:31:28.270 --> 00:31:31.060
terms for this because
they are so unhelpful.

00:31:31.060 --> 00:31:33.070
What's really needed is--

00:31:33.070 --> 00:31:34.180
here's the acid test.

00:31:34.180 --> 00:31:37.890
For those of you who
are going to try stuff,

00:31:37.890 --> 00:31:39.880
just demand that the
company is working

00:31:39.880 --> 00:31:43.960
with you get you an answer fast
and that they do it scalably.

00:31:43.960 --> 00:31:46.206
And stay away from
what you call it.

00:31:46.206 --> 00:31:47.830
You'll know very
quickly whether or not

00:31:47.830 --> 00:31:49.780
you can find patterns
and relationships

00:31:49.780 --> 00:31:51.101
in your data or not.

00:31:51.101 --> 00:31:51.850
JEN BENNETT: Yeah.

00:31:51.850 --> 00:31:52.750
I think that's a
really great point.

00:31:52.750 --> 00:31:55.176
One of the reasons why I'm
super excited about Sight

00:31:55.176 --> 00:31:56.800
Machine and the
partnership with Google

00:31:56.800 --> 00:31:59.830
is I worked with you
earlier on in my career

00:31:59.830 --> 00:32:02.860
and through a couple of
very different problems

00:32:02.860 --> 00:32:05.680
to test that-- to do
that as a test to say,

00:32:05.680 --> 00:32:10.180
the real problem is not going
in and automating a line

00:32:10.180 --> 00:32:12.880
or in gathering
data from one plant.

00:32:12.880 --> 00:32:15.880
It's really about that problem
around can you really get it

00:32:15.880 --> 00:32:19.560
across multiple and
across different problems

00:32:19.560 --> 00:32:21.520
in a cost-effective way.

00:32:21.520 --> 00:32:24.940
Because truly, that's the
growth that we're seeing,

00:32:24.940 --> 00:32:26.080
the agility that we need.

00:32:26.080 --> 00:32:28.480
That adaptability isn't
developing software

00:32:28.480 --> 00:32:29.830
to solve one specific problem.

00:32:29.830 --> 00:32:31.600
It's really that
secret sauce that you

00:32:31.600 --> 00:32:33.820
have to be able to
build that model

00:32:33.820 --> 00:32:35.890
to allow people to
use data in new ways

00:32:35.890 --> 00:32:37.910
and solve new problems
with that same data.

00:32:37.910 --> 00:32:40.210
And I think we're seeing
this across a number

00:32:40.210 --> 00:32:42.460
of manufacturers
and really just want

00:32:42.460 --> 00:32:45.160
to make sure that people are
aware that these technologies

00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:45.790
exist.

00:32:45.790 --> 00:32:48.910
And they're very
fruitful when applied.

00:32:48.910 --> 00:32:52.790
And I think the last point
is that visibility is crucial

00:32:52.790 --> 00:32:56.740
and that data is the first
step in that journey--

00:32:56.740 --> 00:32:57.680
getting data visible.

00:32:57.680 --> 00:32:59.740
You walked us through
that really nicely--

00:32:59.740 --> 00:33:01.930
visibility, solving some
of those key problems

00:33:01.930 --> 00:33:04.720
around quality,
and then moving up

00:33:04.720 --> 00:33:07.550
into the point at which you can
start to use machine learning.

00:33:07.550 --> 00:33:10.290
But honestly, when we talk
about learning, if you're not

00:33:10.290 --> 00:33:12.929
collecting data and
getting it aggregated

00:33:12.929 --> 00:33:14.970
in a way that can be
meaningful, machine learning

00:33:14.970 --> 00:33:16.642
is not going to help you, right?

00:33:16.642 --> 00:33:17.475
JON SOBEL: Right on.

00:33:17.475 --> 00:33:20.230
JEN BENNETT: You need to
take these steps to get data.

00:33:20.230 --> 00:33:22.110
And so that's really key.

00:33:22.110 --> 00:33:23.520
Jon, it's been really great.

00:33:23.520 --> 00:33:26.897
We're going to go and start
our Q&amp;A, so stay tuned.

00:33:26.897 --> 00:33:28.230
We'll be back in about a minute.

00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:45.150
So thanks for joining us.

00:34:45.150 --> 00:34:46.934
And we have some
really great questions.

00:34:46.934 --> 00:34:49.279
We're really excited,
so let's get started.

00:34:49.279 --> 00:34:50.820
First of all, Jon,
the first question

00:34:50.820 --> 00:34:52.739
that came in-- what is
the technical insight

00:34:52.739 --> 00:34:54.780
behind the Sight
Machine solution?

00:34:54.780 --> 00:34:57.120
Is it just advanced
ETL for manufacturing?

00:34:57.120 --> 00:34:58.620
So can you give us
a little bit of--

00:34:58.620 --> 00:34:59.280
JON SOBEL: I love this question.

00:34:59.280 --> 00:35:00.410
JEN BENNETT: This
is insider game.

00:35:00.410 --> 00:35:02.493
JON SOBEL: Somebody knows
what they're doing here.

00:35:02.493 --> 00:35:05.110
So yeah, the question is,
is it an advanced ETL?

00:35:05.110 --> 00:35:06.430
The short answer is yes.

00:35:06.430 --> 00:35:09.480
And I'm not an
engineer, so I want

00:35:09.480 --> 00:35:12.090
to be careful not to get out
over my skis too much here.

00:35:12.090 --> 00:35:16.620
But it's a unified
way of doing ETL

00:35:16.620 --> 00:35:18.730
on the fly for the
manufacturing environment.

00:35:18.730 --> 00:35:21.480
So instead of having to go
build a new model every time

00:35:21.480 --> 00:35:25.200
we encounter a new
process or facility,

00:35:25.200 --> 00:35:28.350
we configure a very
sophisticated stack

00:35:28.350 --> 00:35:31.230
that invokes ETL that's
basically been prebuilt

00:35:31.230 --> 00:35:33.022
but can be configured
for that environment.

00:35:33.022 --> 00:35:34.980
There's a lot of other
pieces you need to have.

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:37.405
We're moving a lot of zeros
and ones around between Edge

00:35:37.405 --> 00:35:39.030
and Cloud and all
kinds of other stuff.

00:35:39.030 --> 00:35:40.170
But that question
is a great question

00:35:40.170 --> 00:35:41.878
because it goes to
the heart of it, which

00:35:41.878 --> 00:35:45.840
is having a flexible,
abstracted data

00:35:45.840 --> 00:35:48.060
model that serves every
manufacturing environment.

00:35:48.060 --> 00:35:50.850
And I think I'll just offer
this comment for our viewers.

00:35:50.850 --> 00:35:53.790
This is how we're going to do
it in a bunch of industries.

00:35:53.790 --> 00:35:55.860
The horizontal platforms
that are out there

00:35:55.860 --> 00:35:57.810
are great for aggregating data.

00:35:57.810 --> 00:36:00.630
But if you want to understand
farming or buildings or cities

00:36:00.630 --> 00:36:02.160
or health care,
there needs to be

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:04.777
some smarts that's built
into the middle of the stack.

00:36:04.777 --> 00:36:06.360
And that's what this
question goes to.

00:36:06.360 --> 00:36:08.280
Great question.

00:36:08.280 --> 00:36:09.690
JEN BENNETT: The next question--

00:36:09.690 --> 00:36:13.320
how do you specifically get
data from the wide variety

00:36:13.320 --> 00:36:14.994
of PLC manufacturers?

00:36:14.994 --> 00:36:16.410
And the question
was, I understand

00:36:16.410 --> 00:36:18.120
you can make a generic API.

00:36:18.120 --> 00:36:20.730
But that doesn't actually
get the data off the PLCs

00:36:20.730 --> 00:36:21.900
and into--

00:36:21.900 --> 00:36:26.110
JON SOBEL: So this is another
really good technical question.

00:36:26.110 --> 00:36:28.320
Let's zoom up really high here.

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:30.340
Three jobs to make data useful--

00:36:30.340 --> 00:36:33.420
extract it, analyze
it, present it somehow.

00:36:33.420 --> 00:36:35.264
This is a question
about extraction.

00:36:35.264 --> 00:36:37.680
And we're partnering with some
great automation companies.

00:36:37.680 --> 00:36:38.740
We get asked this all the time.

00:36:38.740 --> 00:36:40.470
So people in the
controls environment

00:36:40.470 --> 00:36:42.630
are used to very
rigid data structures.

00:36:42.630 --> 00:36:44.822
And the intuition that
most of us have is,

00:36:44.822 --> 00:36:47.030
oh, I got to work with all
that stuff inside of that.

00:36:47.030 --> 00:36:50.910
Actually, all you need to
do is pull that data off

00:36:50.910 --> 00:36:54.120
if you can get a digital
data stream off of a PLC.

00:36:54.120 --> 00:36:57.000
We start to transform the
data once we pull it off.

00:36:57.000 --> 00:36:59.220
So we just need to get it off.

00:36:59.220 --> 00:37:04.937
And 80% of PLCs in North America
are made by the top three PLC

00:37:04.937 --> 00:37:05.520
manufacturers.

00:37:05.520 --> 00:37:08.824
So once you know how to talk
to Siemens, Allen-Bradwell--

00:37:08.824 --> 00:37:09.990
JEN BENNETT: Bradley, yeah--

00:37:09.990 --> 00:37:11.323
JON SOBEL: Yeah, Allen-Bradley--

00:37:11.323 --> 00:37:13.170
Rockwell, Allen-Bradley,
or Mitsubishi,

00:37:13.170 --> 00:37:16.020
you got 80% of them.

00:37:16.020 --> 00:37:17.820
So it's about
extracting the data,

00:37:17.820 --> 00:37:20.070
not trying to work with
inside the controls code,

00:37:20.070 --> 00:37:22.640
and then transforming
it through what we do.

00:37:22.640 --> 00:37:23.800
JEN BENNETT: I think the
other point that you made,

00:37:23.800 --> 00:37:25.890
which I think is really
important-- yes, there's

00:37:25.890 --> 00:37:28.270
data coming off of the
machines and the PLC data.

00:37:28.270 --> 00:37:30.480
There's also a lot of
other data floating around

00:37:30.480 --> 00:37:33.360
the manufacturing
shop floor that

00:37:33.360 --> 00:37:35.020
may not be coming from a PLC.

00:37:35.020 --> 00:37:38.250
And so I think part of the
value of the Sight Machine

00:37:38.250 --> 00:37:40.590
solution built on
Google Cloud is really

00:37:40.590 --> 00:37:43.020
being able to handle
that variety of data.

00:37:43.020 --> 00:37:45.960
It's not just time series data
off the machine and alarm data

00:37:45.960 --> 00:37:46.920
off the machine.

00:37:46.920 --> 00:37:49.300
So I think that's also
important to keep in mind.

00:37:49.300 --> 00:37:50.341
JON SOBEL: And well said.

00:37:50.341 --> 00:37:53.520
Let's just hammer
the point here.

00:37:53.520 --> 00:37:56.310
It would be very
dangerous to build

00:37:56.310 --> 00:37:59.670
a solution around one piece of
automation or one data source.

00:37:59.670 --> 00:38:01.590
Data is coming off of operators.

00:38:01.590 --> 00:38:03.260
It's in quality systems.

00:38:03.260 --> 00:38:05.790
It's in your automation
and controls.

00:38:05.790 --> 00:38:08.520
And we've seen this
wave of solutions

00:38:08.520 --> 00:38:10.930
that's been built for
data of a certain type.

00:38:10.930 --> 00:38:12.430
But the way Google
thinks about data

00:38:12.430 --> 00:38:14.530
and the we think about
data is data is data.

00:38:14.530 --> 00:38:18.190
And so we ought to be
getting a solution that's

00:38:18.190 --> 00:38:20.020
open and agnostic.

00:38:20.020 --> 00:38:22.150
And companies are
very smart about this.

00:38:22.150 --> 00:38:25.120
They do not want to be locked
into XYZ Company's data

00:38:25.120 --> 00:38:28.170
platform that only goes with
that company's equipment.

00:38:28.170 --> 00:38:30.340
That's a horrible business
model for a customer.

00:38:30.340 --> 00:38:31.810
Great business model
if you can get away

00:38:31.810 --> 00:38:33.430
with it selling the
equipment, but it's not

00:38:33.430 --> 00:38:34.554
going to work in a factory.

00:38:34.554 --> 00:38:35.770
There's too many things.

00:38:35.770 --> 00:38:37.889
So I think you're dead on.

00:38:37.889 --> 00:38:38.680
JEN BENNETT: Great.

00:38:38.680 --> 00:38:42.310
Next question-- what tools,
technology platforms are

00:38:42.310 --> 00:38:45.160
available to specifically
support organizations

00:38:45.160 --> 00:38:47.900
in highly-regulated
environments-- for example,

00:38:47.900 --> 00:38:49.959
medical devices, pharma--

00:38:49.959 --> 00:38:52.000
to really help begin this
digital transformation?

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:54.430
JON SOBEL: So
highly-regulated industries

00:38:54.430 --> 00:38:58.360
are probably one of the
best places to start.

00:38:58.360 --> 00:39:03.730
The need for traceability
from end to end--

00:39:03.730 --> 00:39:06.070
so if we think about
highly-regulated industries,

00:39:06.070 --> 00:39:08.080
it's usually something
where there's

00:39:08.080 --> 00:39:11.920
some risk if the product
isn't built right-- so drugs,

00:39:11.920 --> 00:39:14.710
increasingly food,
medical device.

00:39:14.710 --> 00:39:17.110
All of this is
production we want

00:39:17.110 --> 00:39:20.050
to understand holistically and
with a great degree of control

00:39:20.050 --> 00:39:21.940
and granularity.

00:39:21.940 --> 00:39:23.830
There are a bunch
of disparate tools

00:39:23.830 --> 00:39:25.510
out there right now
as people prepare

00:39:25.510 --> 00:39:28.220
for increasing regulation.

00:39:28.220 --> 00:39:30.670
There's a lot of
point solutions.

00:39:30.670 --> 00:39:33.580
What's needed and what I
think you're starting to see

00:39:33.580 --> 00:39:35.530
is a way to knit
together all these data

00:39:35.530 --> 00:39:37.840
sources without having
to go change historians

00:39:37.840 --> 00:39:40.180
or various compliance techniques
the company have used.

00:39:40.180 --> 00:39:43.110
So you can think of all of these
islands of tools that exist.

00:39:43.110 --> 00:39:46.750
And we need some water to
connect it up, and we're there.

00:39:46.750 --> 00:39:51.070
If we get the data out
of all of these islands,

00:39:51.070 --> 00:39:52.870
we can do very powerful
things with it.

00:39:52.870 --> 00:39:54.910
That is exactly what a
regulator wants to see.

00:39:54.910 --> 00:39:57.100
A regulator wants
to know, can I see

00:39:57.100 --> 00:40:01.780
how this batch was made so that
I know that this batch is bad

00:40:01.780 --> 00:40:03.100
but the other ones are good.

00:40:03.100 --> 00:40:04.450
Can I see that data?

00:40:04.450 --> 00:40:05.845
You already have all that data.

00:40:05.845 --> 00:40:07.030
JEN BENNETT: And you know,
I think one of the things--

00:40:07.030 --> 00:40:08.446
I have a real
passion around this,

00:40:08.446 --> 00:40:09.650
and I know you do as well.

00:40:09.650 --> 00:40:13.300
But this idea that not
too distant in the future,

00:40:13.300 --> 00:40:16.090
we're going to be connecting
up all of these data sources

00:40:16.090 --> 00:40:18.070
so that things like recalls--

00:40:18.070 --> 00:40:20.260
I know that we will
look back and say,

00:40:20.260 --> 00:40:23.530
how crazy it was how we found
out about recalls, right?

00:40:23.530 --> 00:40:26.920
We just heard about this
cluster of things that happened

00:40:26.920 --> 00:40:28.210
in a particular population.

00:40:28.210 --> 00:40:30.640
And then it takes months
to get to the point

00:40:30.640 --> 00:40:33.700
where-- the manufacturing side
is just one small piece of it.

00:40:33.700 --> 00:40:35.770
As we start to really
bring together data,

00:40:35.770 --> 00:40:38.470
I think these
patterns, this ability

00:40:38.470 --> 00:40:42.160
to really find
problems quicker is

00:40:42.160 --> 00:40:44.950
going to be game
changing in our world.

00:40:44.950 --> 00:40:47.650
JON SOBEL: I have a
colleague-- our CMO, Syed Hoda,

00:40:47.650 --> 00:40:49.900
always talks about
how when Samsung

00:40:49.900 --> 00:40:54.304
had terrible problems with its
batteries, you get on a plane.

00:40:54.304 --> 00:40:55.720
And the first thing
they'd say is,

00:40:55.720 --> 00:40:57.490
don't bring this
device on the plane.

00:40:57.490 --> 00:40:59.740
And the whole world was
marketing against this quality

00:40:59.740 --> 00:41:00.280
problem.

00:41:00.280 --> 00:41:01.930
Unfortunately, it
took several months

00:41:01.930 --> 00:41:03.610
to figure out what went wrong.

00:41:03.610 --> 00:41:06.550
What you just said is going
to change how companies

00:41:06.550 --> 00:41:08.410
get control of these problems.

00:41:08.410 --> 00:41:10.490
And we're going to
look back one day

00:41:10.490 --> 00:41:13.220
and go, man, that
was the Stone Ages.

00:41:13.220 --> 00:41:13.970
JEN BENNETT: Yeah.

00:41:13.970 --> 00:41:15.678
Probably have time
for one more question.

00:41:15.678 --> 00:41:18.160
So we don't own many
of our own plants,

00:41:18.160 --> 00:41:20.110
but use contract manufacturers.

00:41:20.110 --> 00:41:22.194
How can you drive change
across the virtual supply

00:41:22.194 --> 00:41:23.443
chain involving many partners?

00:41:23.443 --> 00:41:25.120
I know you know
this really well.

00:41:25.120 --> 00:41:26.995
JON SOBEL: I mean, we
both love this question

00:41:26.995 --> 00:41:30.070
because the supply
chain is really

00:41:30.070 --> 00:41:31.840
the right frame of reference.

00:41:31.840 --> 00:41:34.510
This requires some cooperation.

00:41:34.510 --> 00:41:36.130
I think good supply
chains already

00:41:36.130 --> 00:41:37.990
have trust and cooperation.

00:41:37.990 --> 00:41:40.720
Those supply chains
that can handle it

00:41:40.720 --> 00:41:42.740
simply need to share data.

00:41:42.740 --> 00:41:48.050
We have seen some manufacturers
in the middle of the supply

00:41:48.050 --> 00:41:51.299
chain generate huge amounts
of credibility and confidence

00:41:51.299 --> 00:41:53.090
in their production
because they share data

00:41:53.090 --> 00:41:53.965
with their customers.

00:41:53.965 --> 00:41:55.716
So our very first customer--

00:41:55.716 --> 00:41:57.590
we started our company
in Southeast Michigan.

00:41:57.590 --> 00:42:01.580
Our very first customer
was bending metal,

00:42:01.580 --> 00:42:06.170
selling to the big
three, and realized

00:42:06.170 --> 00:42:08.607
that if they showed data
about their production

00:42:08.607 --> 00:42:10.190
and the control over
quality they had,

00:42:10.190 --> 00:42:11.731
that would be a
competitive advantage

00:42:11.731 --> 00:42:12.990
in their supply chain.

00:42:12.990 --> 00:42:16.310
They were getting 7% or 8%
of the bids that they put in.

00:42:16.310 --> 00:42:19.430
And once they revealed
the data, they

00:42:19.430 --> 00:42:23.390
were able to get all kinds of
high-value, higher-margin work

00:42:23.390 --> 00:42:24.950
because they generated trust.

00:42:24.950 --> 00:42:26.340
So this is good for everybody.

00:42:26.340 --> 00:42:28.400
If you're confident in your
production, share your data.

00:42:28.400 --> 00:42:29.660
If you're the head
of the supply chain,

00:42:29.660 --> 00:42:31.440
ask your partners to
share it with you.

00:42:31.440 --> 00:42:32.815
We can all be
better if we do it.

00:42:32.815 --> 00:42:33.720
It's not easy.

00:42:33.720 --> 00:42:36.267
It's a little scary sometimes,
but that's how you get good.

00:42:36.267 --> 00:42:38.600
JEN BENNETT: And I think,
too, that manufacturers have--

00:42:38.600 --> 00:42:40.190
this is something
that is becoming

00:42:40.190 --> 00:42:43.140
more and more important is
around getting that data.

00:42:43.140 --> 00:42:45.030
It really is critical.

00:42:45.030 --> 00:42:47.410
And really having suppliers--

00:42:47.410 --> 00:42:49.890
giving them a preferred
supplier if you

00:42:49.890 --> 00:42:51.140
have that better integration--

00:42:51.140 --> 00:42:53.556
I think these are things from
a business model perspective

00:42:53.556 --> 00:42:55.170
that you can start
to look at as well.

00:42:55.170 --> 00:42:56.794
JON SOBEL: One last
comment real quick.

00:42:56.794 --> 00:42:59.120
There's all kinds of
fights about ownership.

00:42:59.120 --> 00:43:00.230
You own your own data.

00:43:00.230 --> 00:43:01.040
It's your data.

00:43:01.040 --> 00:43:01.820
We can share it.

00:43:01.820 --> 00:43:02.550
We can use it.

00:43:02.550 --> 00:43:04.770
We can put up a license
regime around it.

00:43:04.770 --> 00:43:06.277
This is a totally
solvable problem.

00:43:06.277 --> 00:43:07.610
JEN BENNETT: Yeah, no, we agree.

00:43:07.610 --> 00:43:09.447
And we have one
minute, actually.

00:43:09.447 --> 00:43:11.530
So I'm going to take this
last one really quickly.

00:43:11.530 --> 00:43:14.360
Our organization does not have
many data science resources--

00:43:14.360 --> 00:43:15.830
we talk a lot about this, Jon--

00:43:15.830 --> 00:43:18.706
especially at plant locations.

00:43:18.706 --> 00:43:20.330
How can we drive
better decision making

00:43:20.330 --> 00:43:22.430
without these capabilities?

00:43:22.430 --> 00:43:25.700
I'll quickly jump in and say,
from a Google perspective,

00:43:25.700 --> 00:43:28.130
we understand that
data science resources

00:43:28.130 --> 00:43:29.942
are very difficult to get.

00:43:29.942 --> 00:43:31.400
And so one of the
things that we're

00:43:31.400 --> 00:43:33.230
trying to do with our
technology as well

00:43:33.230 --> 00:43:35.360
is to provide
better tooling that

00:43:35.360 --> 00:43:39.350
enables the democratization
of data science capabilities

00:43:39.350 --> 00:43:40.810
and so making that
more attainable.

00:43:40.810 --> 00:43:42.560
But maybe you can give
us a quick insight.

00:43:42.560 --> 00:43:45.000
JON SOBEL: I love this question.

00:43:45.000 --> 00:43:47.840
We went to a plant a year
ago at a very large company

00:43:47.840 --> 00:43:49.700
that had 200 data
scientists on the line.

00:43:49.700 --> 00:43:51.710
I called up our CTO and
said, uh oh, can we help them

00:43:51.710 --> 00:43:52.550
understand anything?

00:43:52.550 --> 00:43:53.800
They have 200 data scientists.

00:43:53.800 --> 00:43:54.740
He said, good news.

00:43:54.740 --> 00:43:57.200
They don't know anything if
they have 200 data scientists.

00:43:57.200 --> 00:43:58.770
You don't need that many.

00:43:58.770 --> 00:44:00.530
And if companies like
us do our job right,

00:44:00.530 --> 00:44:03.710
we're going to give you really
helpful, useful insight.

00:44:03.710 --> 00:44:05.650
There's a real range.

00:44:05.650 --> 00:44:07.070
A few gets a lot done.

00:44:07.070 --> 00:44:08.690
And if you want
to invest in more,

